543. Diameter of Binary Tree

LeetCode - Diameter of Binary Tree

My Approach

The first thing I needed to understand was height.

For any node:

height = max(left height, right height) + 1

Then I thought about the diameter.

For a particular node, the longest path passing through that node is:

left height + right height + 2

The +2 comes from the two edges connecting the current node to the deepest nodes of its left and right subtrees.

The important realization was:

To calculate the diameter at a node, I need the height of its left and right subtrees.
But while calculating those heights, I can also calculate the best diameter already found inside those subtrees.

So instead of returning only one value from recursion, I created a Diapair object containing:

h → height of the subtree
dia → maximum diameter found in that subtree
How the Recursion Works

For every node:

Recursively get the Diapair from the left subtree.
Recursively get the Diapair from the right subtree.
Calculate the diameter passing through the current node:

left height + right height + 2

The best diameter for the current subtree is the maximum of:
diameter through the current node
left subtree's diameter
right subtree's diameter
Calculate the current subtree's height and return both values together.

So the recursive call doesn't just say:

"Here is my height."

It says:

"Here is my height, and here is the best diameter I found while calculating it."

The Interesting Part

The most interesting part for me was seeing how the Diapair object carries information back through the recursive calls.

A subtree produces one object containing two different answers:

        Diapair
        /     \
     height   diameter

The parent then uses the child's height to calculate its own diameter, while also comparing the child's already-calculated diameter.

So the same recursion is solving two related problems at once.

Complexity

Every node is visited exactly once.

Time: O(n)

Space: O(h) for the recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree.

Takeaway

The main lesson from this problem was:

When a recursive problem needs multiple pieces of information from a subtree, return them together instead of calculating them separately.

Here, height helps the parent calculate the diameter, while diameter preserves the best answer found inside the subtree.

The final code is quite simple once that relationship between height and diameter becomes clear.